#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd1ba2536264e122c84377a1892c3ca86dd8f273b2eab9ba0e2c53eeb504ef65.png)\n\n  \n\nFind a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\sin{\\theta}\\,$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq  \\pi,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\sin{\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = -\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171896 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5777905c-e581-5454-bdbe-29878ad1237b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a55339eae9b193813ab696538c85696ad5f4267e2e31513bdb2c8998cd711311.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})=1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting our integral using the above identities, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}((0+1)-(-1+0)) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
